--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components distributed with Zanshin
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The application jar and the container image built from `Dockerfile` bundle the
following components. Their own licenses apply to them, not Zanshin's.

Apache License 2.0
  org.springframework.boot:*                Spring Boot 4.1
  org.springframework:*                     Spring Framework
  org.hibernate.orm:*                       Hibernate ORM and community dialects
  com.fasterxml.jackson.core:*              Jackson
  org.apache.pdfbox:pdfbox                  PDFBox
  org.apache.httpcomponents.client5:*       Apache HttpClient 5
  org.flywaydb:*                            Flyway
  com.github.docker-java:*                  docker-java
  org.xerial:sqlite-jdbc                    SQLite JDBC driver

BSD 3-Clause / Eclipse Distribution License 1.0
  org.eclipse.jgit:*                        JGit and its Apache SSH transport
  org.postgresql:postgresql                 PostgreSQL JDBC driver (BSD-2-Clause)

MIT
  org.bouncycastle:bcprov-jdk18on           Bouncy Castle provider
  org.bouncycastle:bcpkix-jdk18on           Bouncy Castle PKIX

LGPL-2.1
  org.mariadb.jdbc:mariadb-java-client      MariaDB Connector/J

GPL-2.0 WITH Universal-FOSS-Exception-1.0
  com.mysql:mysql-connector-j               MySQL Connector/J

  Two of the four JDBC drivers Zanshin ships are copyleft, and both are
  `runtimeOnly` in the build: no compiled Zanshin class links against a driver,
  and the connection is obtained through the JDBC API. MySQL Connector/J is
  distributed under the GPL with Oracle's Universal FOSS Exception, whose list of
  permitted licenses includes Apache-2.0 — which is what makes shipping it inside
  an Apache-2.0 image lawful. Replacing that license with a copyleft-incompatible
  one, or linking a driver at compile time, breaks that and is the reason this
  paragraph exists rather than a bare list.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scanners invoked as containers
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Zanshin runs each scanner as an ephemeral container, by command line, in a
separate process. It bundles none of them and links against none of them; the
images are pulled at run time from their own publishers.

  Syft, Grype           Anchore                   Apache-2.0
  checkov               Prisma Cloud/Bridgecrew   Apache-2.0
  gitleaks              Zachary Rice              MIT
  Semgrep OSS           Semgrep, Inc.             LGPL-2.1

The Semgrep *rules* are a separate question from the Semgrep engine, and the
one place where a licence decides how the code is shaped. Zanshin bundles a
single rule of its own and redistributes no third-party rule set. An operator
who wants real coverage has Zanshin fetch `opengrep/opengrep-rules` — LGPL-2.1
with a Commons Clause, accepted explicitly because that clause takes whoever
adopts those rules out of open source in the OSI sense. The rules travel from
their author to that installation; nothing containing them is shipped here.
`semgrep/semgrep-rules` is refused outright, by constant rather than by
convention, because its terms forbid distribution at all. See
`RuleCatalogue` and decision 0006.

Vulnerability and lifecycle data reached over the network — the EPSS scores
(FIRST.org), the CISA KEV catalog and endoflife.date — is neither bundled nor
redistributed, and carries its publishers' own terms of use.
